\date{2025-12-12T05:24:31Z}
\import{common}
\title{Spectral sequence of a tower of fibrations}
\author{trebor}

\p{
  Given a tower of maps #{X_n \to \cdots \to X_1 \to X_0 = 1}, where we assume finite #{n} for simplicity, we wish to compute the homotopy groups of #{X = X_n} from the knowledge of the homotopy groups of the fibers #{F_n = \operatorname{fib}(X_n \to X_{n-1})}.
}
\p{
  For each fiber sequence #{F_n \to X_n \to X_{n-1}}, there is a long exact sequence of homotopy groups, and we wish to arrange these information suitably so that knowing #{\pi_n (F_p)} allows us to piece together #{\pi_n (X)}. This can be achieved using \em{spectral sequences}. See [this link](https://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/spectral+sequences+in+homotopy+type+theory) for another exposition.
}

\tikzcd{
  && {\pi_\bullet (F_{p+1})} && {\pi_\bullet (F_{p})} \\
  {} & {\pi_\bullet (X_{p+1})} && {\pi_\bullet (X_{p})} && {\pi_\bullet (X_{p-1})} & {}
  \arrow["k"{description}, from=1-3, to=2-2]
  \arrow["k"{description}, from=1-5, to=2-4]
  \arrow[from=2-1, to=2-2]
  \arrow["i"{description}, from=2-2, to=2-4]
  \arrow["{j^{(+1)}}"', from=2-4, to=1-3]
  \arrow["i"{description}, from=2-4, to=2-6]
  \arrow["{j^{(+1)}}"', from=2-6, to=1-5]
  \arrow[from=2-6, to=2-7]
}

\p{
  The main difficulty of analyzing the situation is that #{\pi_n (F_p)} is split into two parts that gets appropriated into #{\pi_{n} (X_p)} and #{\pi_{n-1} (X_{p-1})}. This gives multiple possibilities. The case would be easier if, in the rolled-up long exact sequence, one edge of the triangle is always zero. Then the exact sequence splits into various [short exact sequences](hmlg-000H). Since #{F_p} is a fiber, the natural direction is for #{j} to be zero, implying #{i} is epi and #{k} is mono.
}

\p{
  To do this, we consider \em{collapsing #{i} to its image}, i.e. consider turing #{i} into ##{i' : i (\pi_n (X_p)) \to i (\pi_n (X_{p-1})),} repeatedly. From one angle, this makes #{i} iteratively more surjective. From another angle, in the limit, if we consider #{X_p = X_n} where #{p \ge n}, and #{X_p = 1} where #{p \le 0} (so #{F_p = 1} for out-of-bounds indices), then the maps eventually become ##{\im(\pi_n(X) \to \pi_n(X_p)) \to \im(\pi_n(X) \to \pi_n(X_{p-1})),} which are surjective. If we still have long exact sequences for these maps, then this produces a filtration #{F^p \pi_n (X) = \ker(\pi_n (X) \to \pi_n (X_p))} whose associated graded is known.
}

\p{
  Now we need to construct a long exact sequence at the top:
\tikzcd{
  & {?} \\
  {i^r \pi_n (X_{p+r})} && {i^r \pi_n (X_{p+r-1})} \\
  & {\pi_n (F_p)} \\
  {\pi_n (X_p)} && {\pi_n (X_{p-1})}
  \arrow[from=1-2, to=2-1]
  \arrow["{i}"', from=2-1, to=2-3]
  \arrow[hook, from=2-1, to=4-1]
  \arrow[from=2-3, to=1-2]
  \arrow[hook, from=2-3, to=4-3]
  \arrow["k"{description}, from=3-2, to=4-1]
  \arrow["i"', from=4-1, to=4-3]
  \arrow["j"{description}, from=4-3, to=3-2]
}
  The most straightforward choice is attempting to lift the long exact sequence below. From the perspective of #{k}, we want the question mark to be #{k^{-1}i^r \pi_n (X_{p+r})}. This is sufficient for the bottom-left corner, as the image of this under #{k} is exactly #{\im k \cap \im i^r = \ker i \cap \im i^r}, so this corner is exact.
}

\p{
  However, the top-right map cannot simply be #{j}, as #{j \compose i = 0}, so applying #{j} to the image of #{i^r} is identically zero. With this in mind, the next obvious choice is to have it send #{i^r(x)} to #{j(x)} (i.e. #{j \compose i^{-r}}), since we cannot have any iteration of #{i} in the argument of #{j}. However, this is not well-defined, and introduces an intertermination of #{j \ker (i^r : \pi_n (X_{p+r-1}) \to \pi_n (X_{p-1}))}. To solve this, we simply quotient by that factor in the codomain. (Note that since the triangle isn’t actually closed, but a rolled-up spiral, the indices of #{j} needs a suitable shifting.)
\tikzcd{
  & {\mkern-24mu{\dfrac{k^{-1} \im(i^r : \pi_n (X_{p+r}) \to \pi_n (X_p))}{j \ker(i^r : \pi_{n-1} (X_{p-1}) \to \pi_{n-1} (X_{p-r-1}))}}\mkern-24mu} \\
  {i^r \pi_n (X_{p+r})} && {i^r \pi_n (X_{p+r-1})} \\
  & {\pi_n (F_p)} \\
  {\pi_n (X_p)} && {\pi_n (X_{p-1})}
  \arrow["k"', from=1-2, to=2-1]
  \arrow["{i}"', from=2-1, to=2-3]
  \arrow[hook, from=2-1, to=4-1]
  \arrow["{j i^{-r}}"', from=2-3, to=1-2]
  \arrow[hook, from=2-3, to=4-3]
  \arrow["k"{description}, from=3-2, to=4-1]
  \arrow["i"', from=4-1, to=4-3]
  \arrow["j"{description}, from=4-3, to=3-2]
}
  This is ok, as #{k \compose j = 0}, so the quotiented subgroup is already in the kernel of #{k}. We can now check that the triangle is indeed exact.
}

\p{
  We have constructed a sequence of exact triangles such that for large enough #{r}, they split into short exact sequences. So now the goal is to give a tractible way to compute the object
  ##{E_{n,p,r} = \frac{k^{-1} \im(i^r : \pi_n (X_{p+r}) \to \pi_n (X_p))}{j \ker(i^r : \pi_{n-1} (X_{p-1}) \to \pi_{n-1} (X_{p-r-1}))}.}
  As a sanity check, #{E_{n, p, 0} = \pi_n (F_p)}, being the “initial condition” for our sequence. Note that this is also the only data we have, as #{\pi_n (X_p)} are all unknown. Therefore we want some way to compute #{E_{\bullet,\bullet,r+1}} from the data of #{E_{\bullet,\bullet,r}}. (As is the case for all spectral sequences, the “computation” relies on knowing some differentials, but they are often unique by formal reasons.)
}

\p{
  Removing the unknown components from the diagram, we are left with the following sequence:
\tikzcd{[column sep=small]
  & {E_{n,p+1,r}} && {E_{n,p,r}} && {E_{n,p-1,r}} \\
  \textcolor{gray}{\cdots} && \textcolor{gray}{{i^r \pi_n (X_{p+r})}} && \textcolor{gray}{{i^r \pi_n (X_{p+r-1})}} && \textcolor{gray}{\cdots}
  \arrow[color={gray}, from=1-2, to=2-1]
  \arrow[from=1-4, to=1-2]
  \arrow["k"{description}, color={gray}, from=1-4, to=2-3]
  \arrow[from=1-6, to=1-4]
  \arrow[color={gray}, from=1-6, to=2-5]
  \arrow[color={gray}, from=2-1, to=2-3]
  \arrow[color={gray}, from=2-3, to=1-2]
  \arrow[color={gray}, from=2-3, to=2-5]
  \arrow["{ji^{-r}}"{description}, color={gray}, from=2-5, to=1-4]
  \arrow[color={gray}, from=2-5, to=2-7]
  \arrow[color={gray}, from=2-7, to=1-6]
}
  It’s clear that the only maps we can use are the compositions #{d = j i^{-r} k}. Again, since the triangles isn’t actually closed, #{d} sends #{E_{n, p, r}} to #{E_{n+1, p+r, r}} instead of #{E_{n, p+1, r}} as suggested in the diagram. We immediately notice that by exactness of the original triangles, #{d^2 = j i^{-r} \cancel{k j} i^{-r} k = 0}. Therefore #{E_{n,p,r}} forms a chain complex. We can calculate at #{E_{n, p, r}}:
  ##{\begin{aligned}
    \ker d &= k^{-1} \{ i^r (x) \mid x \in \pi_n (X_{p+r}), j(x) = 0 \} \\
    \im d &= j \{ x \in \pi_{n-1} (X_{p-1}) \mid \exists y, k(y) = i^r(x) \}.
  \end{aligned}}
  Finally by exactness of the original triangle, #{\ker j = \im i}, and #{\im k = \ker i}. Therefore the homology of #{d} is exactly #{E_{n,p,r+1}}.
}

\p{
  Summarizing with the indices aligned with convention, given a tower of maps #{X = X_n \to \cdots \to X_1 \to X_0 = 1} between simply connected spaces, we have a spectral sequence
  ##{E^2_{p,q} = \pi_{p+q} (F_q) \Rightarrow \pi_{p+q} (X).}
  With some more work, we can deal with infinite towers and non-simply connected spaces, with sufficiently nice hypotheses.
}
